  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 238 AM EDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nFor the middle and late parts of the week, Georgia will remain \npositioned between a vertically stacked low pressure system \nmeandering along the Mid-Atlantic and New England coasts and an \nintensifying upper level ridge over the Great Plains. Northwesterly \nflow aloft will persist between these features while a broad surface \nhigh will be centered over the eastern CONUS and southeast Canada. \nThis high pressure regime will contribute to precipitable water \nvalues of 0.5 inch or less and rain chances of near 0% through the \nend of the week and into the weekend. Plenty of sunshine of \nsubsidence under the high will also lead to gradually warming \ntemperatures. High temperatures on Wednesday will be in the mid to \nupper 70s on Wednesday afternoon, with some low 80s creeping into \ncentral Georgia on Thursday and Friday. Strong radiational cooling \nwith mostly clear skies and fairly light winds will keep \ntemperatures brisk during the overnight hours, with lows in the mid \n40s to near 50 each morning. \n\nBy the weekend, the axis of the upper ridge will slide further east, \nbecoming centered closer to the forecast area and bringing a \nreinforcing shot of drier air to the area. Furthermore, the surface \nhigh will strengthen and move east of the Appalachians. At this \npoint, a wedge of high pressure will spread into north Georgia, \nleading to breezy easterly winds and relatively cooler temperatures \non Saturday. The lack of rain and plenty of sunshine will help dry \nout finer fuels, so the development of fire danger concerns will \nneed to be monitored as the week goes on.",
